{"id":"housing-by-place","title":"Where new homes are proposed, approved and built","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Which districts take the new housing, which see almost none proposed, and where do proposals get filed and then fail?","finding":"Since 2015 projects adding 68,290 homes were filed; District 6, District 5, District 9 account for 62.7% of them. Proposals run from 21 homes per 1,000 residents in District 1 to 418 in District 6. Of the homes in projects with a recorded outcome, 29.3% were in projects denied or withdrawn. Across neighborhoods, proposals per resident show a weak link with income (-0.39).","how":"Homes proposed = net new homes on Planning project records opened since 2015, placed by parcel. A project counts as approved if the project record or any of its approvals says so, denied if any says disapproved, withdrawn if the project was withdrawn or cancelled. Homes built = net units completed since 2015 in the Planning Department’s housing-production table. Population and income are Census 2020-24.","limits":"Where homes are proposed is set first by zoning and by land that is free to build on: the eastern districts hold the former industrial land and the tall-building zones, and much of the west side is zoned for houses. Few proposals in a district is therefore mainly a zoning fact, not a decision on any application; who set the zoning is a separate question. Many project records carry no written outcome, so the lost-homes share covers only those that do; one very large project can make a district’s whole figure, and a project withdrawn and filed again under a new number is counted each time. The hearing minutes, roll-call votes and appeal decisions now being collected would show who voted, who objected and what reason was recorded in each case.","columns":[{"k":"area","t":"Place","type":"text"},{"k":"pop","t":"Residents","type":"int"},{"k":"income","t":"Typical household income","type":"usd"},{"k":"homesProposed","t":"Homes proposed since 2015","